<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#strateia">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Strateia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Strateia was a term in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> for enrolment in state, military or church service and its obligations, the analogue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> militia. In middle Byzantine times it chiefly meant the duty of a stratiotes either to serve as a soldier himself or to pay for a soldier's upkeep, alone or with co-givers. By the 10th century the duty was attached to military lands, with strateiai for sailors, infantry, cavalry and, from the 960s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, and emperors restricted the sale of such lands. By the 11th century it had become a purely fiscal obligation whose proceeds increasingly paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The holder of such a duty (<b>strateia</b>) served in person or paid for a soldier, sometimes jointly with others, and was exempt from most taxes besides the land tax; in law the stratiotai were distinguished from ordinary farmers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
